@model JobAndOrganisationViewModel
@*
    Where a position puts somebody, and who it answers to. D28 A, D28 D, D30, D31 and its addendum.

    THE CHAIN IS READ-ONLY AND THAT IS THE POINT. It is computed from the position's ancestors as
    at the date and stored on the employee nowhere — D30. Rendering it as a field would invite
    somebody to disagree with it, and there is nothing to disagree with: it is what the chart says.

    REPORTS TO AND LINE MANAGER ARE ONE ROW, not two. They were two, rendered from two models, and
    read as two independent facts — which invited a reader to wonder how a post could report to one
    place and a person answer to another. They are one fact: the post above, and whoever answers
    for it. The row names the post and its state, then the person.

    One partial because four screens show it — Create, the employee page, the Placement screen and
    Change position — and four copies would come to disagree about what "who does this person
    answer to" means. Editable decides whether it offers a choice or states what is true; nothing
    else differs between them.

    IN Shared RATHER THAN BESIDE THE PLACEMENT SCREENS, because two controllers render it: a
    partial in one controller's folder is invisible to the other.
*@
@if (Model?.Position is null)
{
    <div class="mb-3" id="job-organisation-none">
        <span class="text-muted">@T["Choose a position to see where it sits."]</span>

        @if (Model?.LineManager is not null)
        {
            @* The line manager says what it will do rather than going silent. It lives inside the
               "Reports to" row below once there is a position — but there is no row yet, and a
               control that simply vanished would read as a form that forgot to ask. *@
            <div class="form-text" id="line-manager-waiting">@T["Follows the position."]</div>
        }
    </div>

    return;
}

@{ var position = Model.Position; var lineManager = Model.LineManager; }

<dl class="row mb-3" id="job-organisation">
    <dt class="col-sm-3">@T["Organisation"]</dt>
    <dd class="col-sm-9" id="job-organisation-chain">
        @if (position.Chain.Count == 0)
        {
            @* Real, and shown. A position the customer has not placed in the business yet is what
               the Add form's "Positions can't be placed yet" warning leaves behind. *@
            <span class="text-warning" id="job-organisation-unplaced">
                @T["This position is not placed in the organisation yet, so nothing is derived from it."]
            </span>
        }
        else
        {
            @* One row per ancestor, each labelled with the tenant's OWN name for that kind of unit
               — Division, Department, Region. Never four hard-coded words: decision D28 D, because
               a chart with Regions and Branches is as ordinary as one with Divisions, and FRS
               Rule 6 lets a customer rename either. *@
            @foreach (var step in position.Chain)
            {
                <div class="job-organisation-step" data-record-id="@step.RecordId">
                    <span class="text-muted small job-organisation-label">@step.DimensionName:</span>
                    <span class="job-organisation-name">@step.Name</span>
                </div>
            }
        }
    </dd>

    @* ---- ONE ROW: the post above, its state, and who answers for it ----------------------

        An if-chain rather than a switch. Razor compiles either, but a `case` label sitting in a
        markup body is read as text by anything scanning this file — including the localisation
        gate, which is right to be suspicious of a bare word on its own line.
    *@
    <dt class="col-sm-3">@T["Reports to"]</dt>
    <dd class="col-sm-9" id="job-organisation-reports-to">
        @if (position.ReportsTo.Kind == ReportsToKind.Top)
        {
            <span class="text-muted" id="job-reports-to-top">@T["Nobody — top of the reporting line"]</span>
        }
        else if (position.ReportsTo.Kind == ReportsToKind.Vacant)
        {
            @* VACANT IS NOT "ANSWERS TO NOBODY", and saying so was the defect this row was rebuilt
               for. The post is named and marked empty — somebody reading this is the person who
               would fill it — and whoever is above the gap is named below as acting. *@
            <span id="job-reports-to-vacant">@T["{0} (vacant)", position.ReportsTo.PositionName]</span>
        }
        else if (position.ReportsTo.Kind == ReportsToKind.OneHolder)
        {
            @* The post first, then the person: the post is the fact, the person answers for it. *@
            <span id="job-reports-to-one">
                @T["{0} — {1} (line manager)",
                    position.ReportsTo.PositionName, position.ReportsTo.Holders[0].Name]
            </span>
        }
        else
        {
            @* D28 A: the post and its count, never one of the holders. Any rule that chose would
               invent a line manager the customer never stated, silently. *@
            <span id="job-reports-to-several">
                @T["{0} ({1} holders)", position.ReportsTo.PositionName, position.ReportsTo.Holders.Count]
            </span>
        }

        @if (lineManager is not null)
        {
            <partial name="_LineManagerChoice" model="Model" />
        }
    </dd>

    @if (position.Establishment is { } establishment)
    {
        <dt class="col-sm-3">@T["Headcount"]</dt>
        <dd class="col-sm-9" id="job-organisation-headcount">
            <span>@T["{0} approved", establishment.Approved]</span>
            <span class="text-muted mx-1" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span>@T["{0} filled", establishment.Filled]</span>

            @if (position.IsFull)
            {
                @* D26 F: a full position is OFFERED with a warning and never withheld. Refusing it
                   would stop the product recording what an organisation has actually done, which is
                   not the engine's call to make. The warning is the point: somebody should know. *@
                <span class="badge text-bg-warning ms-2" id="job-organisation-full">
                    @T["Already full — this would over-fill it"]
                </span>
            }
        </dd>
    }
</dl>
